::ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A school fundraiser has a $120 setup cost and earns $8 per item sold. Which expression represents profit after x items?
{
= 8x-120#Correct. Profit is revenue minus the fixed setup cost.
~ 120x+8#This treats the setup cost as a per-item rate.
~ 8x+120#This gives total revenue plus cost, not profit.
~ 120-8x#This subtracts earnings from the cost, reversing the profit relationship.
}

::ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A data model predicts 42.6 students will attend a workshop. What is the most reasonable interpretation?
{
~ Exactly 42.6 students attend#People must be counted with whole numbers, so this is not realistic.
= About 43 students attend#Correct. The model output should be rounded to a reasonable whole-number count.
~ About 426 students attend#This moves the decimal point and greatly changes the prediction.
~ No students attend#The prediction is positive, so zero does not match the model.
}

::ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which choice best describes a useful model for a real-world situation?
{
~ A model that ignores units#Units are essential for interpreting the result.
~ A model that always gives whole numbers#Some contexts use decimals, such as money, measurements, or rates.
= A model that fits the context and gives interpretable results#Correct. A useful model must connect mathematics to the situation.
~ A model that never needs checking#Every model should be checked against data, constraints, and assumptions.
}

::ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A population grows by 6% each year from an initial value of 500. Which model is appropriate?
{
~ y=500+0.06x#This is linear growth, not percent growth each year.
~ y=0.06(500)^x#This makes the initial amount part of the base instead of the starting value.
~ y=500(0.94)^x#This represents a 6% decrease, not growth.
= y=500(1.06)^x#Correct. Growth by 6% uses a factor of 1.06 each year.
}

::ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Two models fit a data set. Model A has residuals mostly near 0, while Model B has residuals that form a clear curve. Which model is better supported?
{
= Model A, because residuals near 0 with no pattern suggest a better fit#Correct. Random residuals close to 0 support the model choice.
~ Model B, because a curved residual pattern proves the model is exact#A residual pattern usually means the chosen model misses structure in the data.
~ Model B, because larger residuals are always better#Large residuals indicate larger prediction errors.
~ Neither model can ever be compared#Models can be compared using residuals, context, and prediction accuracy.
}

